{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target frames the §11.3 sustained flag as an intentional feature ('by design') and the failures in SRC_007 and SRC_009 as 'expected oscillation,' but the audit task under §11.3 requires adjudicating whether the flag being sustained is a genuine fault requiring correction. By recharacterizing persistence as 'correctly resisting premature lock-in,' the target blocks the audit from ever reaching a terminal verdict: if convergence is the stopping condition but persistent divergence is simultaneously defined as the system working correctly, no outcome can ever produce a clean audit. This is self-defeating as an adjudication answer because it makes the sustained flag unfalsifiable, which is exactly the kind of failure the audit standard is meant to detect.","counterexample":["If both critics eventually converged on the same verdict, the target would say the system succeeded. If they continued to diverge indefinitely, the target would also say the system succeeded (resisting premature lock-in). No possible state falsifies the claim that the system is working, so the target fails to provide a genuine adjudication of the sustained flag."],"premise":"The audit standard §11.3 requires adjudicating a sustained flag as a fault condition, but the target redefines sustained-flag persistence as a correct-by-design state, eliminating the possibility of ever reaching a clean audit.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"The §11.3 flag is sustained by design: the adjudication ritual is a *stabilizing negative-feedback loop* that prevents premature closure."},{"block":"000000000000","quote":"its persistence indicates the system is correctly resisting premature lock-in."}],"target":"033eab748c73445d9f6c9268a3fc8a1d604b7cb444a4274791ad0653653383a7"}]}